    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate prioritizing high-ceiling matchups, headlined by **Vinnie Pasquantino** and **Seiya Suzuki**, who anchor our lineup with scores of 76.21 and 73.90, respectively. **Vinnie Pasquantino** benefits from a massive park-factor boost and a favorable matchup against Baz, while **Seiya Suzuki** thrives due to a strong platoon advantage against the left-handed Abbott. We are also leaning heavily on the elite speed profiles of **James Wood** (66.26) and **Mickey Moniak** (69.47); both are set to exploit favorable base-running environments against McDonald, whose slower delivery mechanics provide a distinct tactical advantage for our aggressive base-running strategy. **Corbin Carroll** remains an essential start despite a tougher assignment against Sheehan, as his baseline talent maintains a strong 67.54 score.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision involved **Austin Riley**, who narrowly missed the starting cut with a score of 46.66. While **Austin Riley** carries significant power upside, his efficiency was suppressed by a \"Wind In\" risk and a lower-than-ideal spot in the batting order. We opted for the higher floor offered by **Isaac Paredes** and **Kyle Karros** to balance our lineup. Meanwhile, managers should exercise caution with **Brice Turang** (45.98) and **Jose Caballero** (45.56); both face high-skill arms in Skenes and Cavalli. Specifically, **Brice Turang** is up against a elite catcher defense, creating a significant headwind for his stolen base potential. We are monitoring all lineups closely, specifically keeping an eye on **Henry Bolte**, whose 57.88 score is highly dependent on his lower-order placement. Bench stashes like **Edwin Arroyo** and **Cole Young** remain sidelined as they fail to meet our 40-point efficiency floor, preserving our game caps for more optimal matchups.",
